    12	
    13	**Status:** resolved
    14	
    15	## Accuracy is the whole point
    16	
    17	This catalog is a list the user *picks from* to describe real machines in front of them. A
    18	plausible-sounding but non-existent model is worse than an absent one: it silently becomes the
    19	identity that history, records, and the same-model-elsewhere layer are keyed on (D23).
    20	
    21	- [x] **Every model name must be verifiable from the manufacturer's own product listing.** No
    22	      inventing, no "probably exists", no extrapolating a naming pattern to models not seen.
    23	- [x] Where a line is known but individual model names are not, seed the *line* only — do not
    24	      fabricate members. (In practice: nothing was seeded for such lines — see the "not
    25	      enumerated" list in `docs/catalog-sources/README.md`.)
    26	- [x] Record where each manufacturer's data came from, so the next expansion can be audited.
    27	      The three research files live in `docs/catalog-sources/` with a README indexing them.
    28	
    29	## Scope
    30	
    31	- [x] Target ~15–20 manufacturers covering commercial gyms worldwide — **23 shipped**: Life
    32	      Fitness, Hammer Strength, Precor, Cybex, Nautilus, Star Trac, Body-Solid, Technogym, Matrix,
    33	      Gym80, Panatta, Watson, Eleiko, BH Fitness, Hoist, Arsenal Strength, PRIME Fitness, Atlantis
    34	      Strength, Rogue Fitness, Impulse Fitness, Legend Fitness, Sorinex, Titan Fitness.
    35	      (Jerai was not researched and is not seeded.)
    36	- [x] Selectorized, plate-loaded, cable/functional, and Smith/rack where the manufacturer names a
    37	      distinct model
    38	- [x] Each model links to ≥1 seeded exercise; multi-exercise stations link to several
    39	- [x] Add seeded exercises where a model needs one that doesn't exist yet, with correct
    40	      `loadType`, `equipmentTypeTags`, `muscleGroup`
    41	
    42	## Integrity constraints (D24)
    43	
    44	- [x] **Never renumber or reuse an existing catalog UUID** — the 24 shipped models and 12 shipped
    45	      exercises keep their exact ids and names; `version1CatalogIDsAreUnchanged` pins all 36
    46	- [x] New entries get fresh, stable, deterministic UUIDs in the existing `5EED…` scheme
    47	      (dense sequences; allocation is sticky — see `scripts/generate_seed_catalog.py`)
    48	- [x] Bump `version` (1 → 2); the reconciler inserts the new rows into an existing store without
    49	      duplicating, without touching user-created rows, and without disturbing history
    50	- [x] Test: seed at old version → upgrade → new models present, old ids unchanged, user rows intact
    51	      (`upgradeFromVersion1CatalogAddsModelsAndKeepsUserData`, including a user exercise linked to a
    52	      seeded model, D27)
    53	
    54	## Acceptance
    55	
    56	- [x] Catalog is materially larger and every entry traceable to a real product listing
    57	- [x] `xcodebuild test` green (unit + UI): 182 unit tests, 8 UI tests
    58	- [x] App launch time is not visibly degraded by catalog size
    59	
    60	## Outcome
    61	
    62	**Final counts:** catalog version 2 — **74 exercises**, **1887 equipment models**, **23
    63	manufacturers** (from 12 / 24 / 8). Exercise ids 1–74, model ids 1–1887; the next expansion
    64	continues at exercise 75 / model 1888.
    65	
    66	**Generation:** `scripts/catalog_data.py` (content) + `scripts/generate_seed_catalog.py`
    67	(id allocation, invariants, rendering). `--check` fails if the committed JSON is stale.
    68	
    69	**Launch cost** (in-memory store, simulator): catalog decode 6ms; first-launch seed of 1961 rows
    70	270ms (one time); every subsequent launch **0.8ms** via a new fast path in `CatalogSeeder`
    71	(version matches and the seeded row counts match ⇒ nothing to do). Without that fast path the
    72	steady-state reconcile costs ~70ms per launch, so the fast path is ~90× cheaper. Partial stores
    73	still heal: a missing row changes the count and drops through to the full pass.
    74	
    75	**Thin / lower-confidence manufacturers** (re-research before the next expansion):
    76	- **Atlantis Strength** (68 models) — dealer-sourced only; atlantisstrength.com blocked every fetch.
    77	- **Titan Fitness** (1) and **Sorinex** (13, racks only) — essentially unresearched.
    78	- **Star Trac** (8) and **Eleiko** (20) — thin because little exists under those brands. Star Trac's
    79	  strength lines ship as Nautilus today and were deliberately *not* reconstructed by rebranding the
    80	  Nautilus lists; Eleiko's "strength machines" are resold Precor and were not filed under Eleiko.
    81	- **Cybex VR1/VR3/Prestige** and **Life Fitness Signature Series** — names exact, lines incomplete.
    82	  The Signature Series gap is the most commercially significant one.
    83	- **Arsenal Strength**, **Impulse Fitness**, **Panatta** — authorised-dealer sources carrying the
    84	  manufacturer's own SKU codes.
    85	
    86	**Known wart:** three version-1 model names could not be verified verbatim by the research
    87	(`Signature Series Shoulder Press`, `Signature Series Lat Pulldown`, `Discovery Series Shoulder
    88	Press`). They keep their v1 names anyway — the UUID is what history is keyed on, and renaming them
    89	would mislabel already-logged sets.
    90	
    91	**Follow-on noticed while testing:** with ~1900 models the pickers rely on search to reach a row;
    92	the UI tests now search rather than expecting a row on screen. Ticket 21's grouping work should
    93	assume the lists are long.
     1	# 21 — Browse and categorize machines
     2	
     3	**What to build:** Once ticket 20 lands, a flat alphabetical list of several hundred models is
     4	unusable — both when picking a model while adding a machine, and when scanning your own gym's
     5	machines. Add categorization and filtering.
     6	
     7	**Blocked by:** 20 (the categories only matter at scale). Can be built against the current catalog
     8	and will scale with it.
     9	
    10	**Status:** resolved
    11	
    12	## Where it applies
    13	
    14	- [x] **Model picker** (adding/editing a machine): group by manufacturer, and filter by exercise /
    15	      body area and by equipment type (selectorized, plate-loaded, Smith/rack). Search must
    16	      match manufacturer *and* model, so "hammer incline" finds the right row.
    17	- [x] **Gym detail** (your own machines): group by exercise or body area, so a 30-machine gym is
    18	      scannable. Keep the flat A–Z as an option — a small gym doesn't need grouping.
    19	- [x] **Exercises tab**: filter by body area / equipment type, since ticket 20 grows this list too.
    20	
    21	## Data
    22	
    23	- [x] Exercises already carry `muscleGroup` in the seed schema — use it rather than inventing a
    24	      parallel taxonomy, and make sure ticket 20 populates it for every seeded exercise.
    25	- [x] Equipment type is derivable from the linked exercises' `equipmentTypeTags`; if that proves
    26	      too indirect for grouping, add an explicit field to the model schema and note it in ticket 02.
    27	      **It is not derivable** — see Outcome; an explicit `EquipmentModel.equipmentType` was added
    28	      and noted in ticket 02.
    29	- [x] Whatever the user picks (grouping mode, active filters) should persist between visits — this
    30	      is a per-user preference, not per-session state.
    31	
    32	## Constraints
    33	
    34	- [x] Grouping and filtering are **display concerns only** — they must not change what gets logged,
    35	      how snapshots are captured, or how records group (D23). No filter state may leak into a
    36	      logged entry.
    37	- [x] User-created models and exercises appear alongside seeded ones in every grouping, clearly
    38	      marked (D24), and are never filtered out by a category they lack — put them in an
    39	      "Uncategorized" group rather than hiding them.
    40	
    41	## Acceptance
    42	
    43	- [x] Picking a model in a several-hundred-model catalog takes seconds, not scrolling
    44	- [x] Unit tests for the grouping/filtering logic (pure, in `Domain/`)
    45	- [x] `xcodebuild test` green (unit + UI): 206 unit tests, 9 UI tests
    46	
    47	## Outcome
    48	
    49	**Equipment type is an explicit field, not derived.** The ticket's fallback was needed: a model's
    50	loading mechanism is a property of the *machine*, and the linked exercises' `equipmentTypeTags`
    51	describe the *movement*. 1147 of 1887 seeded models derive exactly one tag (`machine`), which
    52	cannot separate a selectorized chest press from a plate-loaded one from a Smith variant — the split
    53	the user actually wants. So `EquipmentModel.equipmentType: EquipmentCategory?` was added (schema +
    54	seed JSON + generator, noted in ticket 02), with catalog **version 3** carrying the values onto
    55	already-seeded stores through the D24 allowlist.
    56	
    57	**How 1887 models were classified without guessing:** `scripts/catalog_data.py` was already
    58	organised under the section headings the ticket-20 research was transcribed from ("Insignia Series
    59	(selectorized)", "Plate-loaded, Iso-Lateral", "Racks & rigs", …). Those headings became `TYPE(...)`
    60	markers — one line per section, applying to the entries below it — plus a third tuple element for
    61	the 165 entries whose section default is wrong (a Smith machine inside a selectorized line, a
    62	functional trainer inside a rack line). Shipped: 893 selectorized, 571 plate-loaded, 216
    63	rack/Smith/bench, 163 cable, 40 bodyweight stations, and **4 honest nils** (Gym80 4401/4402/4403,
    64	Rogue Air Rhino) that browse under "Uncategorized" rather than being invented into a group.
    65	
    66	**Surfaces**
    67	- *Model picker*: grouped by manufacturer / body area / equipment type / flat A–Z, filtered by body
    68	  area and equipment type, searched over "manufacturer + model" with multi-token, order-independent,
    69	  punctuation-insensitive matching ("hammer incline", "iso lateral"). Active filters show as a
    70	  summary row with a one-tap Clear, so a remembered filter can never silently hide the catalog.
    71	- *Gym detail*: machines grouped by exercise or body area, A–Z still the default.
    72	- *Exercises tab*: body-area and equipment-type filters, and rows now show their body area.
    73	- The mid-workout exercise pickers picked up the same multi-token search.
    74	
    75	**Performance:** `CatalogModelIndex` converts the SwiftData rows to value types once per screen
    76	appearance (rebuilt only when the model count changes); every keystroke then filters and groups
    77	plain structs against a precomputed normalised search string. No debounce was needed — filtering
    78	and grouping 2000 rows five times over is asserted under 1s in
    79	`filteringTheWholeCatalogIsFastEnoughToTypeAgainst` and runs in a few ms in practice.
    80	
    81	**Display-only (D23):** all of it lives in `Domain/CatalogBrowsing.swift` as pure functions over
    82	value types, and the remembered state lives in `AppPreferences`. Nothing in the logging, snapshot
    83	or records path reads a grouping mode or a filter.
     1	# Seeded catalog sources
     2	
     3	Provenance for `WorkoutTracker/Resources/SeedCatalog.json` (ticket 20). Each file
     4	is the raw research for a group of manufacturers: the source URLs consulted, the
     5	model names read off them, and a `Confidence` section stating what is complete,
     6	what is thin, and what was deliberately **not** included. A model name that is
     7	not traceable to one of these files does not belong in the catalog (a plausible
     8	but non-existent model silently becomes the identity history is keyed on, D23).
     9	
    10	| File | Manufacturers |
    11	|---|---|
    12	| `group1-…` | Life Fitness, Hammer Strength, Precor, Cybex, Nautilus, Star Trac, Body-Solid |
    13	| `group2-…` | Technogym, Matrix, Gym80, Panatta, Watson, Eleiko, BH Fitness |
    14	| `group3-…` | Hoist, Arsenal Strength, PRIME Fitness, Atlantis Strength, Rogue Fitness, Impulse Fitness, Legend Fitness, Sorinex, Titan Fitness |
    15	
    16	The JSON is generated from `scripts/catalog_data.py` by
    17	`scripts/generate_seed_catalog.py` (`--check` fails if the committed JSON is
    18	stale). Catalog UUID allocation rules are documented in that script's docstring.
    19	
    20	## Traps the research flagged, and what the catalog does about them
    21	
    22	- **Eleiko** resells Precor under its "strength machines" category. Only
    23	  genuinely Eleiko items (cable machines, Prestera racks) are seeded under Eleiko.
    24	- **Star Trac**'s strength lines ship as Nautilus today. Only the 8 Star Trac
    25	  branded names actually sighted are seeded; the Nautilus lists were not rebranded.
    26	- **Matrix** `B`-suffix models are an upholstery trim of the same machine —
    27	  collapsed into one entry. **Gym80** `N`-suffix numbers are distinct SKUs — kept.
    28	- **Hoist** RS-1xxx and RS-2xxx are coexisting generations; only RS-1301,
    29	  RS-1502 and RS-1103 were sighted, so the rest of RS-1xxx is not generated.
    30	- **BH Fitness** names both PL150B and PL155B "Seated Triceps"; the model code in
    31	  the display name is what disambiguates them.
    32	- Where a research file records a *line* without enumerating members (Technogym
    33	  Selection Pro, Gym80 80Classics, Panatta HP Line, Eleiko XF 80, Arsenal BRAVO,
    34	  Impulse IF-series, Sorinex Apex/Dark Horse, Titan T-3/X-3, Cybex legacy Eagle /
    35	  VR2 / Plate Loaded, Nautilus ONE, Life Fitness Circuit Series), nothing is
    36	  seeded for it.
    37	
    38	## Thin / lower-confidence manufacturers
    39	
    40	Worth re-researching before the next expansion:
    41	
    42	- **Atlantis Strength** — dealer-sourced only; atlantisstrength.com refused every
    43	  fetch, so there is no manufacturer corroboration for any of its 68 models.
    44	- **Sorinex** (13, rack configurations only) and **Titan Fitness** (1 machine) —
    45	  effectively unresearched beyond what is listed.
    46	- **Star Trac** (8) and **Eleiko** (20) — thin because there is genuinely little
    47	  to find under those brands, not because research fell short.
    48	- **Cybex VR1 / VR3 / Prestige** and **Life Fitness Signature Series** — names are
    49	  exact but the lines are larger than what could be verified. The Signature
    50	  Series gap is the most commercially significant one: those machines are still
    51	  on thousands of gym floors.
    52	- **Arsenal Strength**, **Impulse Fitness**, **Panatta** — authorised-dealer
    53	  sources carrying the manufacturer's own SKU codes; high confidence, but not
    54	  read off the manufacturer's own site.
     1	"""Seeded equipment catalog content (ticket 20).
     2	
     3	Transcribed from the manufacturer research kept in `docs/catalog-sources/`. Every
     4	model name here was read off a manufacturer listing, manufacturer PDF, or (where
     5	the research flagged it) an authorised dealer carrying the manufacturer's own SKU
     6	codes. Nothing is extrapolated from a naming pattern: where the research recorded
     7	a *line* but did not enumerate its members, the line is absent here rather than
     8	invented.
     9	
    10	Format: MANUFACTURERS = [(manufacturer, [(modelName, "exerciseKey+exerciseKey"), …])]
    11	Exercise keys are defined in generate_seed_catalog.py.
    12	
    13	Equipment type (ticket 21): a `TYPE(...)` marker applies its type to every entry
    14	that follows it until the next marker — one line per line/section, so the type is
    15	read off the same section headings the research was transcribed under. An entry
    16	that differs from its section carries a third element with its own type (a Smith
    17	machine inside a selectorized line, a functional trainer inside a rack line, …).
    18	Types: "selectorized", "plateLoaded", "cable", "rackOrSmith", "bodyweight";
    19	`TYPE(None)` / a `None` third element means *not established by the research* —
    20	those rows show up under "Uncategorized" rather than being guessed into a group.
    21	
    22	Research caveats honoured (see docs/catalog-sources/*.md "Confidence" sections):
    23	- Eleiko: its "strength machines" category is resold Precor. Only genuinely
    24	  Eleiko items (cable machines, Prestera racks) appear here.
    25	- Star Trac: today's Star Trac strength lines ship as Nautilus. Only the 8
    26	  Star Trac-branded names actually sighted are here; the Nautilus lists were
    27	  NOT rebranded.
    28	- Matrix: `B`-suffix models are an upholstery trim of the same machine —
    29	  collapsed into the single non-B entry.
    30	- Gym80: `N`-suffix part numbers are distinct SKUs — kept separate.
    31	- Hoist: RS-1xxx and RS-2xxx are coexisting generations; only the three RS-1xxx
    32	  codes actually sighted are here.
    33	- BH Fitness: PL150B and PL155B are both "Seated Triceps" on BH's own page —
    34	  disambiguated in the display name so the picker is not ambiguous.
    35	- Atlantis: dealer-sourced only, no manufacturer corroboration (lower confidence).
    36	- Rows shipped in catalog version 1 keep their exact v1 name (their UUID is
    37	  keyed on it, D24), even where the research prefers a different string.
    38	"""
    39	
    40	
    41	def TYPE(equipment_type):
    42	    """Equipment-type marker (ticket 21): everything after it, up to the next
    43	    marker, is of this type unless the entry names its own."""
    44	    return (TYPE_MARKER, equipment_type)
    45	
    46	
    47	TYPE_MARKER = "__equipmentType__"
    48	
    49	EQUIPMENT_TYPES = {"selectorized", "plateLoaded", "cable", "rackOrSmith", "bodyweight"}
    50	

